ink it's just a really important  for all of us  okay well let's uh let's stay with you  molly i want to talk about your  individual experiences  um you're all experienced lockdowns in  different ways and molly uh so year 11  that means of course you had the last  couple of terms of year 10 disrupted and  and it goes on presumably um not sitting  gcses this year getting getting an  assessment or maybe your school is  giving you some exams tell us about it  so um they've said obviously the  government announced they were  exams were cancelled but our school has  decided that we're going to do  smaller scale tests in inside school  that's going to set our  grades for um for our gcses  and how has it been molly um all that  teaching and learning from home how have  you managed that  i mean me certainly i've i've much  preferred working from home because i  feel like once you take yourself out of  the school environment  you can just so much more um you know  you take a step back and you can say  hey i'm just like this is who i am and  these are the things i'm interested in  and to isolate that side of yourself  away from school  has been so beneficial especially for me  um  to see that it's not all about grades on  a piece of paper it's about  the education that you get from things  outside of school  and i think that's been really important  thing for everyone to realize  that um yeah that you can get this  distinction between  um you know those busy environments and  actually staying at home  so i think it's been a really good  experience for me very positive  johnny what's the university experience  been then what did i say uh third year  at  warwick so uh similar pattern then  different experience in terms of what  molly's been through  so your second year disrupted and now it  goes on through  virtually the entirety of your your  third year presumably you had a  a bit of face-to-face teaching a return  to campus to return to university at the  end of last year  at